# The kings and princes of Wales, batch 2 (November 2026): the great kings and princes of Gwynedd and of all Wales.
# Gruffudd ap Llywelyn, Gruffudd ap Cynan, Llywelyn Fawr, Dafydd ap Llywelyn, Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, Dafydd ap Gruffudd.
# Same format and house rules as src/princes.py, which adds LONG and CITES from here to its own.
# Sources checked (November 2026): Brut y Tywysogion (tr. Thomas Jones, 1952); the Dictionary of Welsh Biography;
# the Dictionary of National Biography (1893) on Llywelyn ab Iorwerth; The History of Gruffydd ap Cynan (tr. Arthur
# Jones, 1910); the replies of the Welsh to Archbishop Pecham, November 1282 (Registrum Epistolarum Johannis Peckham);
# J. Beverley Smith, Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, Prince of Wales (1998); R. R. Davies, The Age of Conquest (1987).

LONG = {}

LONG["gruffudd-ap-llywelyn"] = (
 ["Gruffudd ap Llywelyn is the only king in all the history of Wales who ruled the whole of it. From 1055 until his death in 1063 every kingdom of Wales, Gwynedd, Powys, Deheubarth, Morgannwg and Gwent, answered to him. He beat the English in battle after battle, carried his border east beyond the Dee and the Wye, and made the kings of England deal with him as an equal. The Chronicle of the Princes called him the head and shield and defender of the Britons.",
  "|| Born | about 1010",
  "|| Father | Llywelyn ap Seisyll, king of Gwynedd and Deheubarth",
  "|| Mother | Angharad, daughter of Maredudd ab Owain",
  "|| King of Gwynedd and Powys | 1039–1063",
  "|| King of all Wales | 1055–1063",
  "|| Great victories | Rhyd-y-groes, 1039; Hereford, 1055; the Machawy, 1056",
  "|| Wife | Ealdgyth, daughter of Earl Ælfgar of Mercia",
  "|| Court | Rhuddlan",
  "|| Died | 5 August 1063",
  "## The slow starter",
  "Gruffudd was the son of Llywelyn ap Seisyll, who had ruled Gwynedd and Deheubarth in years the chronicle remembers for their plenty, and of Angharad, the daughter of the great king Maredudd ab Owain. Walter Map, a writer from the border in the next century, tells that as a young man Gruffudd sat idle by the fire, until one New Year’s Eve he heard a cook say that a piece of meat kept rising to the top of the pot however often it was pushed down. Gruffudd took it as a sign that he too would rise, and from that night he was never idle again.",
  "## Rhyd-y-groes",
  "In 1039 Gruffudd became king of Gwynedd and Powys. In the same year he met the Mercians at Rhyd-y-groes, a ford on the Severn near Welshpool, and destroyed their army. Edwin, the brother of Earl Leofric of Mercia, fell in the battle. For the next quarter of a century no English army could stand against him.[[cite:dwb-gruffudd-ap-llywelyn,brut-jones]]",
  "## King of all Wales",
  "Gruffudd spent sixteen years bringing the south under his rule. In 1055 he won Deheubarth, and in the same year he took Morgannwg and Gwent. For the first and only time, one Welsh king ruled every part of Wales.[[cite:dwb-gruffudd-ap-llywelyn]]",
  "That autumn he joined with Ælfgar, the exiled earl of Mercia, and marched on Hereford. Earl Ralph came out against him with an army mounted in the French manner, and the Welsh broke it. Gruffudd took Hereford and burned it. In June 1056 Bishop Leofgar of Hereford led another English army against him, and at the Machawy, near Glasbury on the Wye, the bishop fell with his men.[[cite:dwb-gruffudd-ap-llywelyn]]",
  "Then Earl Harold, Earl Leofric and Bishop Ealdred came to make peace, and Gruffudd met King Edward the Confessor. He swore to be a faithful under-king, and in return the English left him the lands he had won, east of Offa’s Dyke and across the Dee. He married Ealdgyth, the daughter of his ally Ælfgar, and held court at Rhuddlan, above the river Clwyd. Twenty years after his death, Domesday Book still named him King Gruffudd in the lands he had ruled on the border.",
  "## Head, shield and defender",
  "At Christmas 1062 Earl Harold, the future King Harold, made a sudden attack on Rhuddlan. Gruffudd escaped by sea. In the summer of 1063 Harold came again with a fleet along the coast while his brother Tostig marched in from the east, and the Welsh were worn down in a long and hard campaign. On 5 August 1063 Gruffudd was killed by his own men, and his head was sent to Harold.",
  "The Chronicle of the Princes gave him the most famous farewell in its pages. Gruffudd ap Llywelyn, it says, the head and shield and defender of the Britons, fell; the man who had been until then unbeaten, who had won countless spoils and victories, and who had been rich in gold and silver and jewels and purple robes.[[cite:dwb-gruffudd-ap-llywelyn,brut-jones]]",
  "> The head and shield and defender of the Britons. | Brut y Tywysogion, 1063",
  "## What he left",
  "No king ever again ruled all of Wales. Three years after Gruffudd’s death Harold himself fell at Hastings, and the Normans came. But Gruffudd had shown what Wales could be: one land under one king, strong enough to stand against England. His half-brother Bleddyn ap Cynfyn founded the royal house of Powys, and his daughter Nest was the grandmother of Nest ferch Rhys, the princess of Deheubarth."],
 ["Gruffudd ap Llywelyn yw’r unig frenin yn holl hanes Cymru a reolodd y cyfan ohoni. O 1055 hyd ei farw yn 1063 atebai pob teyrnas yng Nghymru, Gwynedd, Powys, Deheubarth, Morgannwg a Gwent, iddo ef. Trechodd y Saeson mewn brwydr ar ôl brwydr, gwthiodd ei ffin i’r dwyrain y tu hwnt i Ddyfrdwy a Gwy, a gwnaeth i frenhinoedd Lloegr ddelio ag ef fel un cydradd. Galwodd Brut y Tywysogion ef yn ben a tharian ac amddiffynnwr y Brytaniaid.",
  "|| Ganwyd | tua 1010",
  "|| Tad | Llywelyn ap Seisyll, brenin Gwynedd a Deheubarth",
  "|| Mam | Angharad, merch Maredudd ab Owain",
  "|| Brenin Gwynedd a Phowys | 1039–1063",
  "|| Brenin Cymru gyfan | 1055–1063",
  "|| Buddugoliaethau mawr | Rhyd-y-groes, 1039; Henffordd, 1055; y Fachawy, 1056",
  "|| Gwraig | Ealdgyth, merch Iarll Ælfgar o Fersia",
  "|| Llys | Rhuddlan",
  "|| Bu farw | 5 Awst 1063",
  "## Y dechreuwr araf",
  "Mab Llywelyn ap Seisyll oedd Gruffudd, a fu’n rheoli Gwynedd a Deheubarth mewn blynyddoedd a gofia’r cronicl am eu llawnder, ac Angharad, merch y brenin mawr Maredudd ab Owain. Mae Walter Map, awdur o’r gororau yn y ganrif wedyn, yn adrodd bod Gruffudd yn ddyn ifanc yn eistedd yn segur wrth y tân, nes iddo glywed cogydd un Nos Galan yn dweud bod darn o gig yn codi i ben y crochan o hyd, waeth faint o weithiau y’i gwthid i lawr. Cymerodd Gruffudd hynny’n arwydd y codai yntau hefyd, ac o’r noson honno ni fu’n segur byth wedyn.",
  "## Rhyd-y-groes",
  "Yn 1039 daeth Gruffudd yn frenin Gwynedd a Phowys. Yn yr un flwyddyn cyfarfu â’r Mersiaid yn Rhyd-y-groes, rhyd ar afon Hafren ger y Trallwng, a dinistrio’u byddin. Syrthiodd Edwin, brawd Iarll Leofric o Fersia, yn y frwydr. Am y chwarter canrif nesaf ni allai’r un fyddin Seisnig sefyll yn ei erbyn.[[cite:dwb-gruffudd-ap-llywelyn,brut-jones]]",
  "## Brenin Cymru gyfan",
  "Treuliodd Gruffudd un mlynedd ar bymtheg yn dwyn y de dan ei reolaeth. Yn 1055 enillodd Ddeheubarth, ac yn yr un flwyddyn cymerodd Forgannwg a Gwent. Am y tro cyntaf a’r unig dro, rheolai un brenin Cymreig bob rhan o Gymru.[[cite:dwb-gruffudd-ap-llywelyn]]",
  "Yr hydref hwnnw ymunodd ag Ælfgar, iarll alltud Mersia, a gorymdeithio ar Henffordd. Daeth Iarll Ralph allan yn ei erbyn â byddin ar gefn ceffylau yn null Ffrainc, a thorrodd y Cymry hi. Cipiodd Gruffudd Henffordd a’i llosgi. Ym mis Mehefin 1056 arweiniodd yr Esgob Leofgar o Henffordd fyddin Seisnig arall yn ei erbyn, ac ar lan y Fachawy, ger y Clas-ar-Wy, syrthiodd yr esgob gyda’i wŷr.[[cite:dwb-gruffudd-ap-llywelyn]]",
  "Yna daeth Iarll Harold, Iarll Leofric a’r Esgob Ealdred i wneud heddwch, a chyfarfu Gruffudd â’r Brenin Edward y Cyffeswr. Tyngodd i fod yn is-frenin ffyddlon, ac yn dâl gadawodd y Saeson iddo’r tiroedd a enillodd, i’r dwyrain o Glawdd Offa a thros Ddyfrdwy. Priododd ag Ealdgyth, merch ei gynghreiriad Ælfgar, a chynhaliai lys yn Rhuddlan, uwchben afon Clwyd. Ugain mlynedd wedi ei farw, roedd Llyfr Domesday yn dal i’w enwi’n Frenin Gruffudd yn y tiroedd a reolodd ar y ffin.",
  "## Pen a tharian ac amddiffynnwr",
  "Adeg y Nadolig 1062 ymosododd Iarll Harold, y Brenin Harold wedi hynny, yn ddisymwth ar Ruddlan. Dihangodd Gruffudd ar y môr. Yn haf 1063 daeth Harold eto â llynges ar hyd yr arfordir tra gorymdeithiai ei frawd Tostig i mewn o’r dwyrain, a blinwyd y Cymry mewn ymgyrch hir a chaled. Ar 5 Awst 1063 lladdwyd Gruffudd gan ei wŷr ei hun, ac anfonwyd ei ben at Harold.",
  "Rhoddodd Brut y Tywysogion iddo’r ffarwél enwocaf yn ei dudalennau. Syrthiodd Gruffudd ap Llywelyn, medd y Brut, pen a tharian ac amddiffynnwr y Brytaniaid; y gŵr a fu hyd hynny heb ei orchfygu, a enillodd ysbail a buddugoliaethau dirifedi, ac a fu’n gyfoethog mewn aur ac arian a gemau a gwisgoedd porffor.[[cite:dwb-gruffudd-ap-llywelyn,brut-jones]]",
  "> Pen a tharian ac amddiffynnwr y Brytaniaid. | Brut y Tywysogion, 1063",
  "## Yr hyn a adawodd",
  "Ni reolodd yr un brenin Gymru gyfan byth wedyn. Dair blynedd wedi marw Gruffudd syrthiodd Harold ei hun yn Hastings, a daeth y Normaniaid. Ond roedd Gruffudd wedi dangos beth allai Cymru fod: un wlad dan un brenin, yn ddigon cryf i sefyll yn erbyn Lloegr. Sefydlodd ei hanner brawd Bleddyn ap Cynfyn dŷ brenhinol Powys, ac roedd ei ferch Nest yn nain i Nest ferch Rhys, tywysoges Deheubarth."])

LONG["gruffudd-ap-cynan"] = (
 ["Gruffudd ap Cynan was born in exile in Ireland, won his kingdom at Mynydd Carn, spent years in a Norman prison, escaped, and came home to drive the Normans out of Gwynedd. He ruled it for almost forty years and left it strong, at peace and full of new churches. He is the only medieval king of Wales whose life was written as a book of its own, and he was the father of Owain Gwynedd and of Gwenllian, the heroine of Kidwelly.",
  "|| Born | about 1055, in Dublin",
  "|| Father | Cynan ab Iago, of the royal line of Gwynedd",
  "|| Mother | Ragnhild, daughter of Olaf, of the Norse kings of Dublin",
  "|| Great victory | Mynydd Carn, 1081",
  "|| King of Gwynedd | 1081; again from 1099 until 1137",
  "|| Wife | Angharad, daughter of Owain ab Edwin",
  "|| Children | Owain Gwynedd, Cadwaladr, Gwenllian and others",
  "|| Died | 1137, aged 82",
  "|| Buried | Bangor Cathedral",
  "## Born in Dublin",
  "Gruffudd’s grandfather Iago ab Idwal had been king of Gwynedd, of the line of Rhodri Mawr, and his father Cynan had grown up in exile in Ireland. There Cynan married Ragnhild, the daughter of Olaf, of the Norse royal house of Dublin, and Gruffudd was born in the city of Dublin. The book of his life says that his mother told him, when he was a boy, who his father was and what a kingdom he had lost.[[cite:historia-gruffudd]]",
  "## Mynydd Carn",
  "In 1075 Gruffudd crossed the sea with ships from Ireland and landed at Abermenai on Ynys Môn to win back his inheritance. Twice he won Gwynedd and twice he lost it. Then in 1081 he joined Rhys ap Tewdwr, king of Deheubarth, who had been driven out of his own kingdom, and at Mynydd Carn in the north of Dyfed the two exiles met their enemies and won one of the great victories of Welsh history. Gruffudd became king of Gwynedd, and Rhys took back Deheubarth.",
  "## The prisoner of Chester",
  "Gruffudd was not king for long. Soon after Mynydd Carn the Norman earl Hugh of Chester lured him to a meeting at Rug, near Corwen, seized him, and carried him off to prison in Chester. The book of his life says he was kept there in chains for twelve years, while the Normans built castles across Gwynedd at Bangor, Caernarfon and Aberlleiniog.",
  "Then a young man from Edeirnion came to Chester, found the old king in the market place in his fetters, lifted him onto his shoulders and carried him away unseen. In 1094 the Welsh rose all across the north, and Gruffudd came back to lead them. The Normans were driven out of Gwynedd, and in 1098, when they returned in force, a Norwegian fleet sailing into the Menai Strait killed their leader, Earl Hugh of Shrewsbury. By 1099 Gruffudd was home on Môn for good.[[cite:historia-gruffudd]]",
  "## The king who built",
  "For the rest of his long life Gruffudd made Gwynedd strong. When Henry I invaded in 1114 he made peace without losing his land. He built churches and gave to the monasteries of Penmon and Ynys Seiriol, and Welsh tradition holds that he brought order to the bards and musicians of Wales. The book of his life gives the best picture of what his rule meant:",
  "> He made Gwynedd glitter then with limewashed churches like the firmament with stars. | The History of Gruffudd ap Cynan, translated by Arthur Jones",
  "## Old age",
  "In his last years Gruffudd was blind, and his sons Owain and Cadwaladr led the warband of Gwynedd. They won the great victory of Crug Mawr in 1136, and in the same year his daughter Gwenllian fell leading an army at Kidwelly. Gruffudd died in 1137 at the age of eighty-two, and was buried in Bangor Cathedral, in a tomb on the left side of the high altar. The poet Meilyr Brydydd sang his elegy.[[cite:historia-gruffudd]]",
  "## The book of his life",
  "The History of Gruffudd ap Cynan was written down in Latin in the twelfth century, probably in the reign of his son Owain Gwynedd, and survives in a Welsh version. No other medieval king of Wales has a life of his own like it. From Gruffudd came Owain Gwynedd, Llywelyn Fawr and Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, and through his daughter Gwenllian the Lord Rhys."],
 ["Ganed Gruffudd ap Cynan yn alltud yn Iwerddon, enillodd ei deyrnas ym Mynydd Carn, treuliodd flynyddoedd yng ngharchar y Normaniaid, dihangodd, a daeth adref i yrru’r Normaniaid allan o Wynedd. Rheolodd hi am bron i ddeugain mlynedd a’i gadael yn gryf, mewn heddwch ac yn llawn eglwysi newydd. Ef yw’r unig frenin Cymreig yn yr Oesoedd Canol yr ysgrifennwyd hanes ei fywyd yn llyfr ar ei ben ei hun, ac ef oedd tad Owain Gwynedd a Gwenllian, arwres Cydweli.",
  "|| Ganwyd | tua 1055, yn Nulyn",
  "|| Tad | Cynan ab Iago, o linach frenhinol Gwynedd",
  "|| Mam | Ragnhild, merch Olaf, o frenhinoedd Llychlynnaidd Dulyn",
  "|| Buddugoliaeth fawr | Mynydd Carn, 1081",
  "|| Brenin Gwynedd | 1081; eto o 1099 hyd 1137",
  "|| Gwraig | Angharad, merch Owain ab Edwin",
  "|| Plant | Owain Gwynedd, Cadwaladr, Gwenllian ac eraill",
  "|| Bu farw | 1137, yn 82 oed",
  "|| Claddwyd | Eglwys Gadeiriol Bangor",
  "## Ganwyd yn Nulyn",
  "Bu taid Gruffudd, Iago ab Idwal, yn frenin Gwynedd, o linach Rhodri Mawr, ac roedd ei dad Cynan wedi’i fagu’n alltud yn Iwerddon. Yno priododd Cynan â Ragnhild, merch Olaf, o dŷ brenhinol Llychlynnaidd Dulyn, a ganed Gruffudd yn ninas Dulyn. Mae llyfr ei fywyd yn dweud bod ei fam wedi dweud wrtho, pan oedd yn fachgen, pwy oedd ei dad a pha deyrnas a gollodd.[[cite:historia-gruffudd]]",
  "## Mynydd Carn",
  "Yn 1075 croesodd Gruffudd y môr â llongau o Iwerddon a glanio yn Abermenai ar Ynys Môn i ennill ei etifeddiaeth yn ôl. Ddwywaith yr enillodd Wynedd a dwywaith y’i collodd. Yna yn 1081 ymunodd â Rhys ap Tewdwr, brenin Deheubarth, a yrrwyd o’i deyrnas ei hun, ac ym Mynydd Carn yng ngogledd Dyfed cyfarfu’r ddau alltud â’u gelynion ac ennill un o fuddugoliaethau mawr hanes Cymru. Daeth Gruffudd yn frenin Gwynedd, a chymerodd Rhys Ddeheubarth yn ôl.",
  "## Carcharor Caer",
  "Nid hir y bu Gruffudd yn frenin. Yn fuan wedi Mynydd Carn denodd yr iarll Normanaidd Hugh o Gaer ef i gyfarfod yn y Rhug, ger Corwen, ei ddal, a’i gludo i garchar yng Nghaer. Mae llyfr ei fywyd yn dweud iddo gael ei gadw yno mewn cadwynau am ddeuddeng mlynedd, tra codai’r Normaniaid gestyll ar draws Gwynedd ym Mangor, Caernarfon ac Aberlleiniog.",
  "Yna daeth gŵr ifanc o Edeirnion i Gaer, cafodd yr hen frenin yn y farchnad yn ei hualau, ei godi ar ei ysgwyddau a’i gludo ymaith heb i neb sylwi. Yn 1094 cododd y Cymry ar draws y gogledd, a daeth Gruffudd yn ôl i’w harwain. Gyrrwyd y Normaniaid o Wynedd, ac yn 1098, pan ddaethant yn ôl yn eu nerth, lladdodd llynges o Norwy a hwyliai i’r Fenai eu harweinydd, Iarll Hugh o Amwythig. Erbyn 1099 roedd Gruffudd adref ym Môn am byth.[[cite:historia-gruffudd]]",
  "## Y brenin a adeiladodd",
  "Am weddill ei oes hir gwnaeth Gruffudd Wynedd yn gryf. Pan ymosododd Harri I yn 1114 gwnaeth heddwch heb golli ei dir. Cododd eglwysi a rhoi i fynachlogydd Penmon ac Ynys Seiriol, ac yn ôl traddodiad Cymru rhoddodd drefn ar feirdd a cherddorion Cymru. Llyfr ei fywyd sy’n rhoi’r darlun gorau o’r hyn a olygai ei deyrnasiad:",
  "> Gwnaeth Wynedd yn ddisglair yna ag eglwysi gwyngalchog fel y ffurfafen â sêr. | Hanes Gruffudd ap Cynan",
  "## Henaint",
  "Yn ei flynyddoedd olaf roedd Gruffudd yn ddall, a’i feibion Owain a Chadwaladr a arweiniai osgordd Gwynedd. Enillasant fuddugoliaeth fawr Crug Mawr yn 1136, ac yn yr un flwyddyn syrthiodd ei ferch Gwenllian yn arwain byddin yng Nghydweli. Bu farw Gruffudd yn 1137 yn wyth deg dau oed, a’i gladdu yn Eglwys Gadeiriol Bangor, mewn bedd ar ochr chwith yr allor fawr. Canodd y bardd Meilyr Brydydd ei farwnad.[[cite:historia-gruffudd]]",
  "## Llyfr ei fywyd",
  "Ysgrifennwyd Hanes Gruffudd ap Cynan yn Lladin yn y ddeuddegfed ganrif, yn nheyrnasiad ei fab Owain Gwynedd yn ôl pob tebyg, ac mae wedi goroesi mewn fersiwn Cymraeg. Nid oes gan yr un brenin Cymreig arall yn yr Oesoedd Canol fuchedd ei hun fel hon. O Ruffudd y daeth Owain Gwynedd, Llywelyn Fawr a Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, a thrwy ei ferch Gwenllian yr Arglwydd Rhys."])

LONG["llywelyn-fawr"] = (
 ["Llywelyn ab Iorwerth is one of only two rulers of Wales whom the Welsh have called Mawr, the Great. For forty years, from 1200 to 1240, he was the master of Gwynedd and the leader of the Welsh. He drove back King John, took Shrewsbury and the castles of the south, wrote his own terms into Magna Carta, and gathered the rulers of Wales to do homage to him. He built the castles of Dolwyddelan, Dolbadarn, Criccieth and Castell y Bere, and the chronicler of Wales felt unworthy to tell the deeds of this second Achilles.",
  "|| Born | 1173, probably at Dolwyddelan",
  "|| Father | Iorwerth Drwyndwn, son of Owain Gwynedd",
  "|| Mother | Marared, daughter of Madog ap Maredudd, king of Powys",
  "|| Wife | Siwan (Joan), daughter of King John",
  "|| Ruled | Gwynedd from 1200; the leader of the Welsh until 1240",
  "|| His titles | Prince of North Wales; from 1230 Prince of Aberffraw and Lord of Snowdon",
  "|| Sons | Gruffudd and Dafydd",
  "|| Died | 11 April 1240, at Aberconwy Abbey",
  "|| His coffin | St Grwst’s Church, Llanrwst",
  "## The grandson of Owain Gwynedd",
  "Llywelyn was the grandson of Owain Gwynedd. His father Iorwerth Drwyndwn died when he was a child, and he grew up away from Gwynedd while his uncles ruled it. He came back as a young man to win his inheritance. In 1194 he beat his uncle Dafydd ab Owain at Aberconwy, and by 1200 he was the master of all Gwynedd.",
  "## The first treaty",
  "On 11 July 1201 King John made peace with Llywelyn and his nobles. It is the earliest written agreement between a king of England and a Welsh ruler to survive. In 1205 Llywelyn married John’s daughter Siwan, Joan, who became Lady of Wales and his partner in the diplomacy of the next thirty years.[[cite:dnb-llywelyn-ab-iorwerth,wiki-llywelyn-fawr]]",
  "## Against King John",
  "In 1211 John turned on him and led a great army into Gwynedd as far as Bangor. Llywelyn sent Siwan to her father to make peace, and she saved her husband’s kingdom, though it cost him the land east of the Conwy. Within a year Llywelyn had won it all back. He made an alliance with King Philip Augustus of France, John’s great enemy, and the Pope freed the Welsh from the interdict that lay on England.[[cite:wiki-llywelyn-fawr]]",
  "In 1215 the barons of England rose against John, and Llywelyn rode with them. He took Shrewsbury, and in December he led the rulers of Wales south and took the castles of Carmarthen, Kidwelly, Llansteffan, Cardigan and Cilgerran in a single campaign. When John put his seal to Magna Carta that summer, three of its clauses were for the Welsh: their lands to be restored, their own law to be kept in Wales, and Llywelyn’s son Gruffudd and the hostages of Wales to be sent home at once.[[cite:wiki-llywelyn-fawr]]",
  "## Aberdyfi, 1216",
  "In 1216 Llywelyn called the rulers of Wales to Aberdyfi. There he shared out the lands of Deheubarth among the heirs of the Lord Rhys, and the princes of Wales did homage to him as their lord. It was the nearest thing to a parliament of Wales for two hundred years. In 1218, at Worcester, the English crown recognised all that he had won.[[cite:wiki-llywelyn-fawr]]",
  "## The castles of the prince",
  "In the 1220s Llywelyn built in stone. Dolwyddelan in the mountains where he was born, Dolbadarn above Llyn Padarn, Criccieth by the sea, Castell y Bere under Cadair Idris and Deganwy at the mouth of the Conwy were all his. They were Welsh castles, built by a Welsh prince to guard a Welsh state, and they still stand.",
  "His court was at Aber, at Garth Celyn on the shore of the Menai, and at Llys Rhosyr and Aberffraw on Môn. Ednyfed Fychan, his distain or steward, ran his government, and his charters and letters went out in the name of a prince who ruled like a king.",
  "## Prince of Aberffraw and Lord of Snowdon",
  "In 1228 he stopped the English justiciar Hubert de Burgh in the valley of Ceri, and in 1231 he swept through the March, burning Montgomery, Radnor, Hay and Brecon and taking Neath, Kidwelly and Cardigan. From 1230 he called himself by a new title: Prince of Aberffraw and Lord of Snowdon, naming the royal seat of Rhodri Mawr’s line on Môn and the mountains at the heart of Gwynedd. In 1234 the Pact of Middle brought a truce that was renewed every year for the rest of his life.[[cite:wiki-llywelyn-fawr]]",
  "## Strata Florida, 1238",
  "Siwan died in 1237, and Llywelyn founded a friary at Llan-faes on Môn where she was buried. The next year he made sure of the future. On 19 October 1238 he gathered the princes and lords of Wales at the abbey of Strata Florida, and there they swore fealty to his son Dafydd as the heir to his place.[[cite:dnb-llywelyn-ab-iorwerth]]",
  "## A second Achilles",
  "Llywelyn took the habit of a Cistercian monk at Aberconwy, the abbey he had favoured all his life, and he died there on 11 April 1240. The chronicler of Wales wrote:",
  "> I am unworthy to narrate the mighty deeds of this second Achilles. | Annales Cambriae, 1240",
  "When Edward I moved the monks of Aberconwy to Maenan to make room for his castle, they took their founder with them. Llywelyn’s stone coffin is in St Grwst’s Church, Llanrwst, today. The Englishman Matthew Paris was the first to call him the Great, and the poets Dafydd Benfras and Prydydd y Moch sang his praise.[[cite:dnb-llywelyn-ab-iorwerth,wiki-llywelyn-fawr]]",
  "## His children and the crowns of Britain",
  "Llywelyn’s sons were Gruffudd, the father of Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, and Dafydd, who followed him. Through his daughters his blood ran into the royal houses of Britain. His daughter Elen was the grandmother of Robert II, the first Stewart king of Scotland. His daughter Gwladus Ddu married Ralph Mortimer, and through the Mortimers King Edward IV claimed descent from Llywelyn the Great to strengthen his claim to the English throne. The royal family of today traces its line to him the same way.[[cite:wiki-llywelyn-fawr,wiki-gwladus]]"],
 ["Llywelyn ab Iorwerth yw un o ddim ond dau reolwr yng Nghymru a alwyd yn Fawr gan y Cymry. Am ddeugain mlynedd, o 1200 hyd 1240, ef oedd meistr Gwynedd ac arweinydd y Cymry. Gyrrodd y Brenin John yn ôl, cipiodd Amwythig a chestyll y de, ysgrifennodd ei delerau ei hun i’r Magna Carta, a chasglodd reolwyr Cymru i dalu gwrogaeth iddo. Cododd gestyll Dolwyddelan, Dolbadarn, Cricieth a Chastell y Bere, a theimlai croniclydd Cymru’n annheilwng i adrodd gweithredoedd yr ail Achil hwn.",
  "|| Ganwyd | 1173, yn Nolwyddelan yn ôl pob tebyg",
  "|| Tad | Iorwerth Drwyndwn, mab Owain Gwynedd",
  "|| Mam | Marared, merch Madog ap Maredudd, brenin Powys",
  "|| Gwraig | Siwan, merch y Brenin John",
  "|| Rheolodd | Wynedd o 1200; arweinydd y Cymry hyd 1240",
  "|| Ei deitlau | Tywysog Gogledd Cymru; o 1230 Tywysog Aberffraw ac Arglwydd Eryri",
  "|| Meibion | Gruffudd a Dafydd",
  "|| Bu farw | 11 Ebrill 1240, yn Abaty Aberconwy",
  "|| Ei arch | Eglwys Sant Grwst, Llanrwst",
  "## Ŵyr Owain Gwynedd",
  "Ŵyr Owain Gwynedd oedd Llywelyn. Bu farw ei dad, Iorwerth Drwyndwn, pan oedd yn blentyn, a magwyd ef i ffwrdd o Wynedd tra rheolai ei ewythrod hi. Daeth yn ôl yn ddyn ifanc i ennill ei etifeddiaeth. Yn 1194 trechodd ei ewythr Dafydd ab Owain yn Aberconwy, ac erbyn 1200 ef oedd meistr Gwynedd gyfan.",
  "## Y cytundeb cyntaf",
  "Ar 11 Gorffennaf 1201 gwnaeth y Brenin John heddwch â Llywelyn a’i uchelwyr. Dyma’r cytundeb ysgrifenedig cynharaf rhwng brenin Lloegr a rheolwr o Gymru sydd wedi goroesi. Yn 1205 priododd Llywelyn â Siwan, merch John, a ddaeth yn Arglwyddes Cymru ac yn bartner iddo yn niplomyddiaeth y deng mlynedd ar hugain nesaf.[[cite:dnb-llywelyn-ab-iorwerth,wiki-llywelyn-fawr]]",
  "## Yn erbyn y Brenin John",
  "Yn 1211 trodd John arno ac arwain byddin fawr i Wynedd cyn belled â Bangor. Anfonodd Llywelyn Siwan at ei thad i wneud heddwch, ac achubodd hi deyrnas ei gŵr, er i hynny gostio iddo’r tir i’r dwyrain o Gonwy. O fewn blwyddyn roedd Llywelyn wedi ennill y cyfan yn ôl. Gwnaeth gynghrair â’r Brenin Philippe Auguste o Ffrainc, gelyn mawr John, a rhyddhaodd y Pab y Cymry o’r gwaharddiad a orweddai ar Loegr.[[cite:wiki-llywelyn-fawr]]",
  "Yn 1215 cododd barwniaid Lloegr yn erbyn John, a marchogodd Llywelyn gyda hwy. Cipiodd Amwythig, ac ym mis Rhagfyr arweiniodd reolwyr Cymru i’r de a chymryd cestyll Caerfyrddin, Cydweli, Llansteffan, Aberteifi a Chilgerran mewn un ymgyrch. Pan seliodd John y Magna Carta yr haf hwnnw, roedd tri o’i gymalau i’r Cymry: eu tiroedd i’w hadfer, eu cyfraith eu hunain i’w chadw yng Nghymru, a Gruffudd fab Llywelyn a gwystlon Cymru i’w hanfon adref ar unwaith.[[cite:wiki-llywelyn-fawr]]",
  "## Aberdyfi, 1216",
  "Yn 1216 galwodd Llywelyn reolwyr Cymru i Aberdyfi. Yno rhannodd diroedd Deheubarth rhwng etifeddion yr Arglwydd Rhys, a thalodd tywysogion Cymru wrogaeth iddo fel eu harglwydd. Dyma’r peth agosaf at senedd i Gymru am ddau gan mlynedd. Yn 1218, yng Nghaerwrangon, cydnabu coron Lloegr y cyfan a enillodd.[[cite:wiki-llywelyn-fawr]]",
  "## Cestyll y tywysog",
  "Yn y 1220au cododd Llywelyn mewn carreg. Ei eiddo ef oedd Dolwyddelan yn y mynyddoedd lle’i ganed, Dolbadarn uwchben Llyn Padarn, Cricieth ar lan y môr, Castell y Bere dan Gadair Idris a Degannwy yn aber Conwy. Cestyll Cymreig oeddent, a godwyd gan dywysog o Gymro i warchod gwladwriaeth Gymreig, ac maent yn sefyll o hyd.",
  "Roedd ei lys yn Aber, yng Ngarth Celyn ar lan y Fenai, ac yn Llys Rhosyr ac Aberffraw ym Môn. Ednyfed Fychan, ei ddistain, a redai ei lywodraeth, ac âi ei siarteri a’i lythyrau allan yn enw tywysog a reolai fel brenin.",
  "## Tywysog Aberffraw ac Arglwydd Eryri",
  "Yn 1228 ataliodd ustus Lloegr, Hubert de Burgh, yn nyffryn Ceri, ac yn 1231 ysgubodd drwy’r Mers, gan losgi Trefaldwyn, Maesyfed, y Gelli a Brycheiniog a chymryd Castell-nedd, Cydweli ac Aberteifi. O 1230 galwai ei hun wrth deitl newydd: Tywysog Aberffraw ac Arglwydd Eryri, gan enwi sedd frenhinol llinach Rhodri Mawr ym Môn a’r mynyddoedd yng nghalon Gwynedd. Yn 1234 daeth Cytundeb Middle â chadoediad a adnewyddwyd bob blwyddyn am weddill ei oes.[[cite:wiki-llywelyn-fawr]]",
  "## Ystrad Fflur, 1238",
  "Bu farw Siwan yn 1237, a sefydlodd Llywelyn frodordy yn Llan-faes ym Môn lle’i claddwyd. Y flwyddyn wedyn sicrhaodd y dyfodol. Ar 19 Hydref 1238 casglodd dywysogion ac arglwyddi Cymru yn abaty Ystrad Fflur, ac yno tyngasant ffyddlondeb i’w fab Dafydd fel etifedd ei le.[[cite:dnb-llywelyn-ab-iorwerth]]",
  "## Ail Achil",
  "Cymerodd Llywelyn abid mynach Sistersaidd yn Aberconwy, yr abaty a ffafriodd ar hyd ei oes, a bu farw yno ar 11 Ebrill 1240. Ysgrifennodd croniclydd Cymru:",
  "> Nid wyf deilwng i adrodd gweithredoedd mawr yr ail Achil hwn. | Annales Cambriae, 1240",
  "Pan symudodd Edward I fynachod Aberconwy i Faenan i wneud lle i’w gastell, aethant â’u sylfaenydd gyda hwy. Mae arch garreg Llywelyn yn Eglwys Sant Grwst, Llanrwst, heddiw. Y Sais Matthew Paris oedd y cyntaf i’w alw’n Fawr, a chanodd y beirdd Dafydd Benfras a Phrydydd y Moch ei glod.[[cite:dnb-llywelyn-ab-iorwerth,wiki-llywelyn-fawr]]",
  "## Ei blant a choronau Prydain",
  "Meibion Llywelyn oedd Gruffudd, tad Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, a Dafydd, a’i dilynodd. Drwy ei ferched llifodd ei waed i dai brenhinol Prydain. Roedd ei ferch Elen yn nain i Robert II, brenin Stewart cyntaf yr Alban. Priododd ei ferch Gwladus Ddu â Ralph Mortimer, a thrwy’r Mortimeriaid hawliodd y Brenin Edward IV ei fod yn disgyn o Lywelyn Fawr er mwyn cryfhau ei hawl i orsedd Lloegr. Mae’r teulu brenhinol heddiw yn olrhain ei linach ato yr un ffordd.[[cite:wiki-llywelyn-fawr,wiki-gwladus]]"])

LONG["dafydd-ap-llywelyn"] = (
 ["Dafydd ap Llywelyn, the son of Llywelyn Fawr and Siwan, was the grandson of King John and the nephew of King Henry III, and he was the first ruler of Wales to make Prince of Wales his formal title. He ruled for only six years, from 1240 to 1246, but in that time he gathered the Welsh behind him, turned to the Pope for help against England, and fought Henry III to a standstill on the Conwy. The Chronicle of the Princes called him the shield of Wales.",
  "|| Born | about 1212",
  "|| Father | Llywelyn Fawr",
  "|| Mother | Siwan, daughter of King John",
  "|| Wife | Isabella de Braose",
  "|| Prince | 1240–1246; Prince of Wales from 1244",
  "|| Died | 25 February 1246, at Abergwyngregyn",
  "|| Buried | Aberconwy Abbey, beside his father",
  "## The chosen heir",
  "Dafydd was born about 1212, the son of Llywelyn Fawr and his wife Siwan. Llywelyn chose him as his heir and worked for twenty years to make sure of it. In 1220 Henry III recognised Dafydd as his father’s heir, Pope Honorius III declared Siwan legitimate, and on 19 October 1238 the princes of Wales gathered at Strata Florida and swore fealty to him.[[cite:dnb-llywelyn-ab-iorwerth,wiki-dafydd-ap-llywelyn]]",
  "## Prince of Wales",
  "When Llywelyn died in 1240, Dafydd went to meet his uncle Henry III at Gloucester, where he was knighted. Henry wanted to break the power his father had built, and in 1241 he marched into Gwynedd and made Dafydd give up the lands beyond the Conwy and hand over his half-brother Gruffudd. On 1 March 1244 Gruffudd died trying to escape from the Tower of London.[[cite:wiki-dafydd-ap-llywelyn]]",
  "Dafydd rose. The rulers of Wales came over to him, and he began to style himself Prince of Wales, the first ruler of Wales to make it his formal title. He sent envoys to King Louis IX of France and to Pope Innocent IV, and offered to hold Wales from the Pope himself, so that no king of England could claim it. For a time the Pope listened.[[cite:wiki-dafydd-ap-llywelyn]]",
  "## The campaign of Deganwy",
  "By the spring of 1245 Dafydd had taken back Mold. In August Henry III came with a great army to the Conwy and began to build a castle at Deganwy. The Welsh cut off his supplies and harried his camp, and an English soldier wrote home that the army lay in its tents watching, fasting, praying and freezing. In the autumn Henry went home without having beaten Dafydd.[[cite:wiki-dafydd-ap-llywelyn]]",
  "## The shield of Wales",
  "On 25 February 1246 Dafydd died suddenly at the royal court of Abergwyngregyn, aged about thirty-three, and he was buried beside his father at Aberconwy. The poet Dafydd Benfras sang his elegy, and the Chronicle of the Princes called him tarian Cymru, the shield of Wales. His nephews Llywelyn and Owain took up the fight, and Llywelyn ap Gruffudd would carry the title Prince of Wales to its height.[[cite:wiki-dafydd-ap-llywelyn,brut-jones]]",
  "> Tarian Cymru: the shield of Wales. | Brut y Tywysogion, 1246"],
 ["Roedd Dafydd ap Llywelyn, mab Llywelyn Fawr a Siwan, yn ŵyr i’r Brenin John ac yn nai i’r Brenin Harri III, ac ef oedd y rheolwr cyntaf yng Nghymru i wneud Tywysog Cymru yn deitl ffurfiol iddo. Dim ond am chwe blynedd y rheolodd, o 1240 hyd 1246, ond yn yr amser hwnnw casglodd y Cymry y tu ôl iddo, trodd at y Pab am help yn erbyn Lloegr, ac ymladdodd Harri III i stop ar lan Conwy. Galwodd Brut y Tywysogion ef yn darian Cymru.",
  "|| Ganwyd | tua 1212",
  "|| Tad | Llywelyn Fawr",
  "|| Mam | Siwan, merch y Brenin John",
  "|| Gwraig | Isabella de Braose",
  "|| Tywysog | 1240–1246; Tywysog Cymru o 1244",
  "|| Bu farw | 25 Chwefror 1246, yn Abergwyngregyn",
  "|| Claddwyd | Abaty Aberconwy, wrth ochr ei dad",
  "## Yr etifedd dewisedig",
  "Ganed Dafydd tua 1212, yn fab i Lywelyn Fawr a’i wraig Siwan. Dewisodd Llywelyn ef yn etifedd iddo a gweithio am ugain mlynedd i sicrhau hynny. Yn 1220 cydnabu Harri III Dafydd yn etifedd ei dad, cyhoeddodd y Pab Honorius III fod Siwan yn gyfreithlon, ac ar 19 Hydref 1238 ymgasglodd tywysogion Cymru yn Ystrad Fflur a thyngu ffyddlondeb iddo.[[cite:dnb-llywelyn-ab-iorwerth,wiki-dafydd-ap-llywelyn]]",
  "## Tywysog Cymru",
  "Pan fu farw Llywelyn yn 1240, aeth Dafydd i gyfarfod â’i ewythr Harri III yng Nghaerloyw, lle’i hurddwyd yn farchog. Roedd Harri am dorri’r grym a adeiladodd ei dad, ac yn 1241 gorymdeithiodd i Wynedd a gorfodi Dafydd i ildio’r tiroedd y tu hwnt i Gonwy a throsglwyddo ei hanner brawd Gruffudd. Ar 1 Mawrth 1244 bu farw Gruffudd wrth geisio dianc o Dŵr Llundain.[[cite:wiki-dafydd-ap-llywelyn]]",
  "Cododd Dafydd. Daeth rheolwyr Cymru drosodd ato, a dechreuodd alw ei hun yn Dywysog Cymru, y rheolwr cyntaf yng Nghymru i’w wneud yn deitl ffurfiol. Anfonodd negeswyr at y Brenin Louis IX o Ffrainc ac at y Pab Innocent IV, a chynigiodd ddal Cymru oddi wrth y Pab ei hun, fel na allai’r un brenin Lloegr ei hawlio. Am gyfnod gwrandawodd y Pab.[[cite:wiki-dafydd-ap-llywelyn]]",
  "## Ymgyrch Degannwy",
  "Erbyn gwanwyn 1245 roedd Dafydd wedi cymryd yr Wyddgrug yn ôl. Ym mis Awst daeth Harri III â byddin fawr at afon Conwy a dechrau codi castell yn Negannwy. Torrodd y Cymry ei gyflenwadau ac aflonyddu ar ei wersyll, ac ysgrifennodd milwr o Sais adref fod y fyddin yn gorwedd yn ei phebyll yn gwylio, yn ymprydio, yn gweddïo ac yn rhewi. Yn yr hydref aeth Harri adref heb drechu Dafydd.[[cite:wiki-dafydd-ap-llywelyn]]",
  "## Tarian Cymru",
  "Ar 25 Chwefror 1246 bu farw Dafydd yn ddisymwth yn llys brenhinol Abergwyngregyn, tua thair ar ddeg ar hugain oed, a’i gladdu wrth ochr ei dad yn Aberconwy. Canodd y bardd Dafydd Benfras ei farwnad, a galwodd Brut y Tywysogion ef yn darian Cymru. Cymerodd ei neiaint Llywelyn ac Owain y frwydr i fyny, a byddai Llywelyn ap Gruffudd yn dwyn y teitl Tywysog Cymru i’w uchafbwynt.[[cite:wiki-dafydd-ap-llywelyn,brut-jones]]",
  "> Tarian Cymru. | Brut y Tywysogion, 1246"])

LONG["llywelyn-ap-gruffudd"] = (
 ["Llywelyn ap Gruffudd is Llywelyn ein Llyw Olaf, Llywelyn our Last Leader. He is the only Welsh prince whom the crown of England ever recognised as Prince of Wales. He won back the lands of his grandfather Llywelyn Fawr, gathered the lords of Wales under him, and for a time ruled a Principality of Wales that stretched from Môn to the edge of Gwent. When Edward I came with the full power of England, Llywelyn would not give up his people, and he fell at Cilmeri on 11 December 1282. Wales has remembered that day ever since.",
  "|| Born | about 1223",
  "|| Father | Gruffudd ap Llywelyn, son of Llywelyn Fawr",
  "|| Mother | Senena",
  "|| Prince of Gwynedd | from 1246; sole ruler from 1255",
  "|| Prince of Wales | from 1258; recognised by England in 1267",
  "|| Wife | Eleanor de Montfort, married 1278",
  "|| Daughter | Gwenllian, born 1282",
  "|| Died | 11 December 1282, near Cilmeri",
  "|| Buried | by tradition, Abbey Cwm-hir",
  "## The grandson of Llywelyn Fawr",
  "Llywelyn was the son of Gruffudd, the eldest son of Llywelyn Fawr, and of Senena. When his uncle Dafydd ap Llywelyn died in 1246, Llywelyn and his brother Owain took up the fight and divided Gwynedd between them, and in 1247 the Treaty of Woodstock shut them into the land west of the Conwy. In 1255, after the battle of Bryn Derwin, Llywelyn became the sole ruler of Gwynedd.[[cite:wiki-llywelyn-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "## The rising of 1256",
  "In 1256 the people of the Four Cantrefs east of the Conwy, worn down by the officials of the Lord Edward, called on Llywelyn to free them. In a single week of November he crossed the Conwy and swept the English out of the land as far as the Dee. Then he turned south, and the Welsh of Ceredigion, Builth and Ystrad Tywi rose with him. In June 1257 an English army under Stephen Bauzan was destroyed in the valley of the Tywi near Llandeilo.[[cite:wiki-llywelyn-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "## Prince of Wales",
  "In 1258 the lords of Wales did homage to Llywelyn, and he took the title Prince of Wales. It appears first in an agreement he made that year with the great lords of Scotland, as one free nation to another. Through the 1260s, while England was torn by the barons’ war, he widened his rule, and allied himself with Simon de Montfort.[[cite:wiki-llywelyn-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "On 29 September 1267, at Montgomery, Henry III put his seal to the treaty that made Llywelyn’s title law. Llywelyn and his heirs were to be Princes of Wales, and the other lords of Wales were to hold their lands of him. It was exactly a hundred years since King Owain Gwynedd had first called himself Prince of the Welsh, and Henry III, whose grandfather had raged at the title, now granted it.[[cite:wiki-llywelyn-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "## Eleanor",
  "Llywelyn was promised to Eleanor de Montfort, the daughter of his old ally Simon, and in 1275 they were married by proxy in France. On her voyage to Wales her ship was seized by Edward I’s men, and she was held prisoner for three years. Llywelyn did not give her up, and on 13 October 1278 they were married at last in Worcester Cathedral. In Wales she was Princess of Wales and Lady of Snowdon.[[cite:wiki-llywelyn-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "## Against Edward I",
  "Edward I, who became king in 1272, was a far harder enemy than his father. In 1277 he invaded Wales with a vast army and a fleet, and by the Treaty of Aberconwy Llywelyn was shut again behind the Conwy. But he kept Gwynedd west of the river, and he kept his title, Prince of Wales.[[cite:wiki-llywelyn-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "On Palm Sunday 1282 his brother Dafydd took Hawarden Castle, and Wales rose. Llywelyn joined the war his people had begun. On 19 June 1282, at the court of Garth Celyn, Eleanor died giving birth to their only child, Gwenllian.",
  "## Since the days of Camber son of Brutus",
  "In November 1282 Archbishop John Pecham of Canterbury came to Garth Celyn to make peace. Edward’s offer was an earldom and a rich estate in England, if Llywelyn would give up Wales for ever. The prince and his council refused. They answered that the land was his inheritance and had been held by his forefathers since the days of Camber son of Brutus, and the people of Eryri gave their own answer.[[cite:senedd-peckham-1282,wiki-llywelyn-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "> Even if the prince desired to give the king seisin of them, they themselves would not do homage to any stranger, of whose language, customs and laws they are utterly ignorant. | The people of Snowdonia to Archbishop Pecham, November 1282",
  "On 6 November 1282, while the talks were still going on, an English force from Môn tried to cross the Menai on a bridge of boats at Moel-y-don. The Welsh fell on them as they came ashore and drove them back into the sea, where many were drowned.[[cite:wiki-llywelyn-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "## Cilmeri",
  "In December Llywelyn went south to raise the men of mid Wales. On 11 December 1282, near Cilmeri by the river Irfon, he was cut off from his army and killed. His head was sent to Edward, crowned in mockery with ivy, and set above the gate of the Tower of London. Welsh tradition holds that the monks of Abbey Cwm-hir buried his body, and a stone marks the place there today.[[cite:wiki-llywelyn-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "## Do you not see the way of the wind and the rain?",
  "The poet Gruffudd ab yr Ynad Coch made an elegy for him that has never been matched in Welsh. In it the whole world seems to be ending: the wind and the rain, the oaks crashing together, the sea flooding the land, the sun hurrying across the sky and the stars fallen.",
  "> Poni welwch-chwi hynt y gwynt a’r glaw? Poni welwch-chwi’r deri’n ymdaraw? | Gruffudd ab yr Ynad Coch, elegy for Llywelyn, 1282",
  "## Ein Llyw Olaf",
  "Llywelyn was the last prince of the house of Aberffraw to rule Wales in his own right. A great granite stone stands now at Cilmeri, near where he fell, and every December people from all over Wales gather there to remember him. His title, Prince of Wales, had been the title of Owain Gwynedd, Dafydd and Llywelyn himself. A hundred and eighteen years after Cilmeri, Owain Glyndŵr would be proclaimed Prince of Wales and take it up again."],
 ["Llywelyn ap Gruffudd yw Llywelyn ein Llyw Olaf. Ef yw’r unig dywysog o Gymro a gydnabuwyd erioed gan goron Lloegr yn Dywysog Cymru. Enillodd yn ôl diroedd ei daid Llywelyn Fawr, casglodd arglwyddi Cymru o dano, ac am gyfnod rheolodd Dywysogaeth Cymru a ymestynnai o Fôn hyd ymyl Gwent. Pan ddaeth Edward I â holl rym Lloegr, ni fynnai Llywelyn ildio’i bobl, a syrthiodd yng Nghilmeri ar 11 Rhagfyr 1282. Mae Cymru wedi cofio’r diwrnod hwnnw byth wedyn.",
  "|| Ganwyd | tua 1223",
  "|| Tad | Gruffudd ap Llywelyn, mab Llywelyn Fawr",
  "|| Mam | Senena",
  "|| Tywysog Gwynedd | o 1246; unig reolwr o 1255",
  "|| Tywysog Cymru | o 1258; cydnabuwyd gan Loegr yn 1267",
  "|| Gwraig | Elinor de Montfort, priodwyd 1278",
  "|| Merch | Gwenllian, ganwyd 1282",
  "|| Bu farw | 11 Rhagfyr 1282, ger Cilmeri",
  "|| Claddwyd | yn ôl traddodiad, Abaty Cwm-hir",
  "## Ŵyr Llywelyn Fawr",
  "Mab Gruffudd, mab hynaf Llywelyn Fawr, a Senena oedd Llywelyn. Pan fu farw ei ewythr Dafydd ap Llywelyn yn 1246, cymerodd Llywelyn a’i frawd Owain y frwydr i fyny a rhannu Gwynedd rhyngddynt, ac yn 1247 caeodd Cytundeb Woodstock hwy yn y tir i’r gorllewin o Gonwy. Yn 1255, wedi brwydr Bryn Derwin, daeth Llywelyn yn unig reolwr Gwynedd.[[cite:wiki-llywelyn-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "## Gwrthryfel 1256",
  "Yn 1256 galwodd pobl y Pedwar Cantref i’r dwyrain o Gonwy, wedi blino ar swyddogion yr Arglwydd Edward, ar Lywelyn i’w rhyddhau. Mewn un wythnos ym mis Tachwedd croesodd Gonwy ac ysgubo’r Saeson o’r tir hyd at Ddyfrdwy. Yna trodd i’r de, a chododd Cymry Ceredigion, Buallt ac Ystrad Tywi gydag ef. Ym mis Mehefin 1257 dinistriwyd byddin Seisnig dan Stephen Bauzan yn nyffryn Tywi ger Llandeilo.[[cite:wiki-llywelyn-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "## Tywysog Cymru",
  "Yn 1258 talodd arglwyddi Cymru wrogaeth i Lywelyn, a chymerodd y teitl Tywysog Cymru. Mae’n ymddangos gyntaf mewn cytundeb a wnaeth y flwyddyn honno ag arglwyddi mawr yr Alban, fel un genedl rydd ag un arall. Drwy’r 1260au, tra rhwygwyd Lloegr gan ryfel y barwniaid, ehangodd ei reolaeth, a chynghreiriodd â Simon de Montfort.[[cite:wiki-llywelyn-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "Ar 29 Medi 1267, yn Nhrefaldwyn, seliodd Harri III y cytundeb a wnaeth deitl Llywelyn yn gyfraith. Roedd Llywelyn a’i etifeddion i fod yn Dywysogion Cymru, ac roedd arglwyddi eraill Cymru i ddal eu tiroedd oddi wrtho ef. Roedd yn union gan mlynedd ers i’r Brenin Owain Gwynedd alw ei hun gyntaf yn Dywysog y Cymry, ac yn awr rhoddodd Harri III, y bu ei daid yn gandryll am y teitl, ei sêl arno.[[cite:wiki-llywelyn-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "## Elinor",
  "Addawyd Llywelyn i Elinor de Montfort, merch ei hen gynghreiriad Simon, ac yn 1275 priodwyd hwy drwy ddirprwy yn Ffrainc. Ar ei mordaith i Gymru cipiwyd ei llong gan wŷr Edward I, a’i chadw’n garcharor am dair blynedd. Ni roddodd Llywelyn hi heibio, ac ar 13 Hydref 1278 priodwyd hwy o’r diwedd yn Eglwys Gadeiriol Caerwrangon. Yng Nghymru hi oedd Tywysoges Cymru ac Arglwyddes Eryri.[[cite:wiki-llywelyn-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "## Yn erbyn Edward I",
  "Roedd Edward I, a ddaeth yn frenin yn 1272, yn elyn llawer caletach na’i dad. Yn 1277 ymosododd ar Gymru â byddin enfawr a llynges, a thrwy Gytundeb Aberconwy caewyd Llywelyn eto y tu ôl i Gonwy. Ond cadwodd Wynedd i’r gorllewin o’r afon, a chadwodd ei deitl, Tywysog Cymru.[[cite:wiki-llywelyn-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "Ar Sul y Blodau 1282 cipiodd ei frawd Dafydd Gastell Penarlâg, a chododd Cymru. Ymunodd Llywelyn â’r rhyfel a ddechreuodd ei bobl. Ar 19 Mehefin 1282, yn llys Garth Celyn, bu farw Elinor wrth eni eu hunig blentyn, Gwenllian.",
  "## Er dyddiau Camber fab Brutus",
  "Ym mis Tachwedd 1282 daeth yr Archesgob John Pecham o Gaergaint i Garth Celyn i wneud heddwch. Cynnig Edward oedd iarllaeth ac ystad gyfoethog yn Lloegr, os ildiai Llywelyn Gymru am byth. Gwrthododd y tywysog a’i gyngor. Atebasant mai ei etifeddiaeth ef oedd y tir, a’i fod wedi’i ddal gan ei gyndadau er dyddiau Camber fab Brutus, a rhoddodd pobl Eryri eu hateb eu hunain.[[cite:senedd-peckham-1282,wiki-llywelyn-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "> Hyd yn oed pe mynnai’r tywysog roi eu meddiant i’r brenin, ni fynnent hwy wneud gwrogaeth i unrhyw ddieithryn, nad ydynt yn gwybod dim am ei iaith, ei arferion na’i gyfreithiau. | Pobl Eryri at yr Archesgob Pecham, Tachwedd 1282",
  "Ar 6 Tachwedd 1282, tra oedd y trafodaethau’n dal i fynd ymlaen, ceisiodd llu Seisnig o Fôn groesi’r Fenai ar bont o gychod ym Moel-y-don. Disgynnodd y Cymry arnynt wrth iddynt ddod i’r lan a’u gyrru’n ôl i’r môr, lle boddodd llawer.[[cite:wiki-llywelyn-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "## Cilmeri",
  "Ym mis Rhagfyr aeth Llywelyn i’r de i godi gwŷr canolbarth Cymru. Ar 11 Rhagfyr 1282, ger Cilmeri ar lan afon Irfon, torrwyd ef oddi wrth ei fyddin a’i ladd. Anfonwyd ei ben at Edward, wedi’i goroni ag eiddew yn wawd, a’i osod uwchben porth Tŵr Llundain. Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru claddwyd ei gorff gan fynachod Abaty Cwm-hir, ac mae carreg yn nodi’r fan yno heddiw.[[cite:wiki-llywelyn-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "## Poni welwch-chwi hynt y gwynt a’r glaw?",
  "Lluniodd y bardd Gruffudd ab yr Ynad Coch farwnad iddo na fu ei hail yn y Gymraeg. Ynddi mae’r byd i gyd fel petai’n dod i ben: y gwynt a’r glaw, y deri’n taro yn erbyn ei gilydd, y môr yn boddi’r tir, yr haul yn rhuthro ar draws yr awyr a’r sêr wedi syrthio.",
  "> Poni welwch-chwi hynt y gwynt a’r glaw? Poni welwch-chwi’r deri’n ymdaraw? | Gruffudd ab yr Ynad Coch, marwnad Llywelyn, 1282",
  "## Ein Llyw Olaf",
  "Llywelyn oedd tywysog olaf tŷ Aberffraw i reoli Cymru yn ei hawl ei hun. Saif carreg wenithfaen fawr yng Nghilmeri bellach, ger y fan lle syrthiodd, a phob mis Rhagfyr daw pobl o bob rhan o Gymru yno i’w gofio. Ei deitl, Tywysog Cymru, oedd teitl Owain Gwynedd, Dafydd a Llywelyn ei hun. Gant a deunaw o flynyddoedd wedi Cilmeri, byddai Owain Glyndŵr yn cael ei gyhoeddi’n Dywysog Cymru ac yn ei gymryd i fyny eto."])

LONG["dafydd-ap-gruffudd"] = (
 ["Dafydd ap Gruffudd, the brother of Llywelyn ein Llyw Olaf, struck the first blow of the last war of the princes of Wales, and after Llywelyn fell at Cilmeri he took up the title Prince of Wales and fought on from the mountains for six more months. He was the last prince of the house of Aberffraw to rule in Wales. When he was taken, Edward I had him put to death at Shrewsbury in a way never used before on a prisoner of his rank.",
  "|| Born | about 1238",
  "|| Father | Gruffudd ap Llywelyn, son of Llywelyn Fawr",
  "|| Mother | Senena",
  "|| Brother | Llywelyn ap Gruffudd",
  "|| Wife | Elizabeth Ferrers",
  "|| Prince of Wales | December 1282 to June 1283",
  "|| Died | 3 October 1283, at Shrewsbury",
  "## The youngest brother",
  "Dafydd was the third of the four sons of Gruffudd ap Llywelyn and Senena, and a grandson of Llywelyn Fawr. He grew up between two worlds, at the court of Gwynedd and at the court of the kings of England, and he knew both well. After the war of 1277 he was given the lordship of Rhufoniog and Dyffryn Clwyd, around Denbigh, by the Treaty of Aberconwy.[[cite:wiki-dafydd-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "## Palm Sunday, 1282",
  "The Welsh of the north-east had borne five years of English officials and English courts in place of their own law. On the night of Palm Sunday 1282 Dafydd led them against Hawarden Castle and took it, and the Welsh rose across the country. It was the beginning of the last war of the princes. His brother Llywelyn joined the war, and through the summer and autumn the Welsh held Eryri against the armies of Edward I.[[cite:wiki-dafydd-ap-gruffudd,wiki-llywelyn-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "## Prince of Wales",
  "When Llywelyn fell at Cilmeri on 11 December 1282, Dafydd took up his title and fought on as Prince of Wales. He held out at Dolwyddelan, then at Castell y Bere under Cadair Idris, which surrendered on 25 April 1283, then at Dolbadarn, and at last in the mountains above Abergwyngregyn, where his grandfather and his brother had held court.[[cite:wiki-dafydd-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "On 22 June 1283 he was found in his last hiding place, Nanhysglain, a hollow in the bog near Bera Mawr, and taken after a fight in which he was badly wounded. His son Owain was taken with him.[[cite:wiki-dafydd-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "## Shrewsbury",
  "Edward called a parliament to Shrewsbury to try him. On 3 October 1283 Dafydd was dragged through the streets, hanged, and cut apart while he still lived, the first man of high rank in recorded history to be put to death in that way. He had fought for his own land, and his last days were spent as its prince.[[cite:wiki-dafydd-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "His sons Llywelyn and Owain were shut away in Bristol Castle, and his daughter Gwladys in a priory in Lincolnshire, like her cousin Gwenllian, Llywelyn’s daughter. With Dafydd ended the rule of the princes of the house of Aberffraw in Wales, but not their story: a hundred years later his kinsman Owain Lawgoch would claim their title from France, and after him Owain Glyndŵr."],
 ["Dafydd ap Gruffudd, brawd Llywelyn ein Llyw Olaf, a drawodd ergyd gyntaf rhyfel olaf tywysogion Cymru, ac wedi i Lywelyn syrthio yng Nghilmeri cymerodd y teitl Tywysog Cymru ac ymladd ymlaen o’r mynyddoedd am chwe mis arall. Ef oedd tywysog olaf tŷ Aberffraw i reoli yng Nghymru. Pan ddaliwyd ef, rhoddodd Edward I ef i farwolaeth yn Amwythig mewn ffordd na ddefnyddiwyd erioed o’r blaen ar garcharor o’i radd.",
  "|| Ganwyd | tua 1238",
  "|| Tad | Gruffudd ap Llywelyn, mab Llywelyn Fawr",
  "|| Mam | Senena",
  "|| Brawd | Llywelyn ap Gruffudd",
  "|| Gwraig | Elizabeth Ferrers",
  "|| Tywysog Cymru | Rhagfyr 1282 hyd Fehefin 1283",
  "|| Bu farw | 3 Hydref 1283, yn Amwythig",
  "## Y brawd ieuengaf",
  "Dafydd oedd y trydydd o bedwar mab Gruffudd ap Llywelyn a Senena, ac yn ŵyr i Lywelyn Fawr. Fe’i magwyd rhwng dau fyd, yn llys Gwynedd ac yn llys brenhinoedd Lloegr, ac roedd yn adnabod y ddau’n dda. Wedi rhyfel 1277 rhoddwyd iddo arglwyddiaeth Rhufoniog a Dyffryn Clwyd, o gwmpas Dinbych, drwy Gytundeb Aberconwy.[[cite:wiki-dafydd-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "## Sul y Blodau, 1282",
  "Roedd Cymry’r gogledd-ddwyrain wedi dioddef pum mlynedd o swyddogion Seisnig a llysoedd Seisnig yn lle eu cyfraith eu hunain. Ar noson Sul y Blodau 1282 arweiniodd Dafydd hwy yn erbyn Castell Penarlâg a’i gipio, a chododd y Cymry ar draws y wlad. Dyna ddechrau rhyfel olaf y tywysogion. Ymunodd ei frawd Llywelyn â’r rhyfel, a thrwy’r haf a’r hydref daliodd y Cymry Eryri yn erbyn byddinoedd Edward I.[[cite:wiki-dafydd-ap-gruffudd,wiki-llywelyn-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "## Tywysog Cymru",
  "Pan syrthiodd Llywelyn yng Nghilmeri ar 11 Rhagfyr 1282, cymerodd Dafydd ei deitl ac ymladd ymlaen yn Dywysog Cymru. Daliodd ei dir yn Nolwyddelan, yna yng Nghastell y Bere dan Gadair Idris, a ildiodd ar 25 Ebrill 1283, yna yn Nolbadarn, ac yn olaf yn y mynyddoedd uwchben Abergwyngregyn, lle bu ei daid a’i frawd yn cynnal llys.[[cite:wiki-dafydd-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "Ar 22 Mehefin 1283 cafwyd ef yn ei guddfan olaf, Nanhysglain, pant yn y gors ger Bera Mawr, a’i ddal wedi ymladd lle clwyfwyd ef yn ddrwg. Daliwyd ei fab Owain gydag ef.[[cite:wiki-dafydd-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "## Amwythig",
  "Galwodd Edward senedd i Amwythig i’w brofi. Ar 3 Hydref 1283 llusgwyd Dafydd drwy’r strydoedd, ei grogi, a’i dorri’n ddarnau tra oedd yn fyw o hyd, y gŵr cyntaf o radd uchel yn yr hanes a gofnodwyd i’w roi i farwolaeth yn y ffordd honno. Roedd wedi ymladd dros ei wlad ei hun, a threuliodd ei ddyddiau olaf yn dywysog arni.[[cite:wiki-dafydd-ap-gruffudd]]",
  "Caewyd ei feibion Llywelyn ac Owain yng Nghastell Bryste, a’i ferch Gwladys mewn priordy yn swydd Lincoln, fel ei chyfnither Gwenllian, merch Llywelyn. Gyda Dafydd daeth rheolaeth tywysogion tŷ Aberffraw yng Nghymru i ben, ond nid eu hanes: gan mlynedd yn ddiweddarach byddai ei berthynas Owain Lawgoch yn hawlio eu teitl o Ffrainc, ac ar ei ôl ef Owain Glyndŵr."])

